🧩 Phase 1 — CNN for Character Recognition

In this phase, a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) is trained on EMNIST characters.

Goal:

Learn to classify individual handwritten characters

What happens:

Input: 28×28 grayscale images
Model: CNN (Conv + Pooling layers)
Output: Character prediction (A–Z / digits)

Result:

Baseline model for character-level recognition
🧩 Phase 2 — Data Preprocessing & Cleaning

Before training deep models, the dataset is cleaned and prepared.

Steps include:

Normalization of pixel values
Noise reduction and thresholding
Image resizing and padding
Dataset balancing and mapping labels to characters

Result:

Clean and structured dataset ready for deep learning models
🧩 Phase 3 — CRNN for Sequence Recognition

This is the core of the OCR system.

Model:

CNN + BiLSTM (CRNN)
Connectionist Temporal Classification (CTC) loss

Goal:

Convert image sequences into character sequences (words)

What happens:

CNN extracts visual features
BiLSTM learns sequence dependencies
CTC aligns predictions without needing character segmentation

Result:

Word-level handwritten recognition
🧩 Phase 4 — OCR Inference System

Final stage where the trained model is used for prediction.

What happens:

Input image → CRNN model → probability sequence
CTC decoder converts output into readable text

Features:

Greedy decoding (CTC)
Image → Word prediction
Visualization of results

Result:

Complete OCR system capable of recognizing handwritten words
🚀 Final Outcome

This project demonstrates a full OCR pipeline:

Character recognition (CNN)
Sequence modeling (CRNN)
End-to-end handwritten word recognition system
